A||||6||GT-AutoX||Got her running, here's how (I think).||||6||
Z||000000||GT-AutoX||05-29-2001||01:06 AM||||Thanks for the help everyone!  I took another look at the plugs and they were completely covered in black residue.  Funny, because they looked fine a mere 75 miles ago.  I also noticed that my coolant temp. sensor plug had come off.  (I need a new sensor because the jack is all busted but Checker sold me one that doesn't fit and can't figure out how to get me the right one because their computer says the wrong one is the right size)  Here's my theory: The ECM thought I was running at -40 degrees all the time due to the infinite coolant temp sensor resistance and richened up the mixture until my plugs finally couldn't take it anymore.  Make sense?  <P>Couple more questions:  How can I test my egr vacuum solenoid?  All I know is I get 1 Megohm at the terminals.<P>Also, what are the sawed off metal pipes bolted to the trunk side of my engine compartment for?||||reg||6
Z||000001||Pontiaddict||05-29-2001||01:58 AM||||The coolant temp thing sounds right.<BR>I don't know about the egr.<BR>The tube is part of some sort of alternator and coil cooling system, there is a fan in the trunk that comes on with the cooling fan and blows cool air on them. <p>[This message has been edited by Pontiaddict (edited 05-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ken Wittlief||05-29-2001||09:20 AM||||You can fake out the ECM temp sensor by putting a 220 ohm resistor across the plug - that will make the ECM think the engine is at 160 or 170° or so - will be right most of the time, better than having it think its at -40° all the time, till you get the sensor replaced.<P>  BTW you are looking at the one that comes out under the EGR solonide, horizonatlly from the block - had to move the solonoide to even see it, right?<P>to test the solonoide, have you looked at the online service manual? If that doesnt help you let me know, I have the factory maunuals from the library at home right now.  I think the first indication is if you are getting an EGR error code.<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/fierov6/OSG/osg.html" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nbci.com/fierov6/OSG/osg.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||05-29-2001||09:51 AM||||Are you sure the parts place is giving you the right sensor -there a at least 3 different temp sensors on a V6. You want the 2 wire jobbie near the thermostat housing||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||05-29-2001||01:49 PM||||Running w/o the sensor can and likely will make it run rich. continued running like that can also ruin the catalyst.<P>Also,,, depending on year... you may have to replace the wireharness connector. I'm not sure on the V6, but I don't think you can get the round connector ECM temp sensor at all anymore. If you have that one, then you have to convert to the new sensor and the new connector. This new connector must be soldered to the harness to make sure the ECM gets a correct reading.<P>This is actually a good thing as the new connector is much more water proof, thus stays clean inside and gives better data to the ECM.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 05-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||87GTZ34||05-29-2001||03:41 PM||||GT, What is the location on eng for the sensor in question?<P>1) on the left, top, rear of the head next to the coil or<BR> <BR>2) up front by the water outlet<P>The reason I ask is, if it is the 1 on the head then that is only for the light/gauge the ECM uses the one by the water outlet. This may explain the parts store's mix-up.||||reg||3
Z||000006||GT-AutoX||05-29-2001||08:48 PM||||The sensor is definitely the ecm coolant temp sensor located near the water pump.  Now I have been to two parts stores and neither can find the right one for me.  The one they keep producing has the right socket, plug, etc. but the thread diameter is too small to fit in my engine block.  What the hell?  Once again its an 86.5 GT.  I just bought it off a "discount" lot.  Could my block be different?  The odo only shows 97k.  I had Checker look through all model years and even some 3.4s but they were all the same sensor.  I think I'll repost this as a new topic.||||reg||1
